        </PAGEOBJECT>
        <PAGEOBJECT XPOS="560.630551181103" YPOS="1966.3580600655" OwnPage="6" ItemID="193666576" PTYPE="4" WIDTH="337.322834645669" HEIGHT="513.070866141732" FRTYPE="0" CLIPEDIT="0" PWIDTH="1" PLINEART="1" LOCALSCX="1" LOCALSCY="1" LOCALX="0" LOCALY="0" LOCALROT="0" PICART="1" SCALETYPE="1" RATIO="1" COLUMNS="1" COLGAP="0" AUTOTEXT="0" EXTRA="0" TEXTRA="0" BEXTRA="0" REXTRA="0" VAlign="0" FLOP="1" PLTSHOW="0" BASEOF="0" textPathType="0" textPathFlipped="0" path="M0 0 L337.323 0 L337.323 513.071 L0 513.071 L0 0 Z" copath="M0 0 L337.323 0 L337.323 513.071 L0 513.071 L0 0 Z" gXpos="560.630551181103" gYpos="60.5312884119576" gWidth="0" gHeight="0" LAYER="0" NEXTITEM="193400144" BACKITEM="-1">
            <StoryText>
                <DefaultStyle/>
                <ITEXT CH="What You Will Find Here — and What You Won't"/>
                <para PARENT="scene_page_subtitle"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="There is one thing that most books about relationships don't say plainly."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="When we're hurting in a relationship, we rarely arrive with a neutral view of what's happening. We arrive with our own pain, our own exhaustion, our own version of the story. And that version — even when it's sincere, even when it's legitimate — is a lens. It illuminates certain things, and leaves others in the shadow."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="This booklet starts from there."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="What It Is"/>
                <para PARENT="scene_page_subtitle"/>
                <ITEXT CH="A tool for awareness. Not therapy. Not a diagnosis. Not a way to prove you're right — or that the other person is wrong."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="It offers six scenes from the ordinary life of a couple. Scenes that everyone recognizes, precisely because they're mundane. And for each scene, an invitation to look at what's really happening — in the dynamic, in the roles, in what each person finds there without admitting it to themselves."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="The theoretical framework used is Transactional Analysis — ego states, psychological games, the Karpman Drama Triangle. You don't need to know these concepts to use this booklet. They are explained as the scenes unfold, in the context where they appear."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="What It Is Not"/>
                <para PARENT="scene_page_subtitle"/>
                <ITEXT CH="This booklet is not couples therapy. It doesn't replace individual or joint support from a professional. If what you're going through is deep, painful, or has been going on for a long time — what you'll find here can be a starting point. Not a complete solution."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="It's also not a tool to convince the other person. If you use it to show them &quot;what they do,&quot; you'll have missed the point entirely. This booklet is first and foremost a mirror — and mirrors look both ways."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="The Most Important Thing to Understand Before Starting"/>
                <para PARENT="scene_page_subtitle"/>
                <ITEXT CH="Imagine the following scene."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="She criticizes him for never talking about what he feels. He closes off a little more with each criticism. She criticizes more, because he closes off. He closes off more, because she criticizes."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="Who started it? The question has no answer — because it's not the right question. What's happening here is not a fault. It's a loop. And in a loop, there is no one to blame — there are two people feeding the same system, each in their own way, without seeing it."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="That is what a psychological game is. Both play. Always."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="It's not intuitive — especially when you're hurting. When you're exhausted, when you're carrying what should be shared, when you feel like you're making efforts the other person isn't making — the natural view is asymmetric. You see what the other person does. You see much less clearly what you yourself do to keep it going."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="It's not bad faith. It's mechanical. Pain, mental load, distress direct your attention toward what hurts — not toward what you're contributing."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="This booklet doesn't ask you to minimize what you're experiencing. It asks something more useful: to understand how you participate in what keeps repeating. Not to burden yourself. Because it's the only place from which you can truly act."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="How to Use This Booklet"/>
                <para PARENT="scene_page_subtitle"/>
                <ITEXT CH="Solo. This is the most natural way to start. You read the scenes, note what you recognize, do the daily tracking. Without the other person knowing, without talking about it. The work happens inside you first — and it produces effects in the relationship, often without needing to be explained."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="Together. If your partner is open to exploring this with you, each scene offers a version for two — a short, structured conversation, outside moments of tension. This is not improvised couples therapy. It's a way of looking together at what you usually look at separately."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="With a guide. If you already work with a coach or therapist, this booklet can serve as support between sessions — to observe, note, and bring something concrete to what you're exploring together."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="One last thing."/>
                <para PARENT="scene_page_subtitle"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="The scenes you're about to read are intentionally ordinary. Nothing dramatic. Nothing exceptional. These are the moments nobody notices — precisely because they repeat."/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <para PARENT="BODY"/>
                <ITEXT CH="That's where the games live. And that's where we can begin to see them."/>
                <trail PARENT="BODY"/>
            </StoryText>
        </PAGEOBJECT>
